---
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro';
import { path } from '../lib/paths';
import { SITE } from '../lib/site';
---

<Page
  title="Code of conduct"
  description="What is expected of contributors to MathemAct, what is not tolerated, and what happens when the line is crossed."
  lead="MathemAct is meant to be a place where mathematicians can describe their experiences with AI openly, including failures, doubts, and uncomfortable results. That only works if people can disagree without making participation personally costly."
  updated="October 2026"
>
  <p class="status">
    This is the short version. The full text is
    <a href={`${SITE.repository}/blob/main/docs/code-of-conduct.md`}>in the repository</a>.
    Where the two differ, the full text governs and this page is what needs fixing.
  </p>

  <h2>Scope</h2>

  <p>
    This code applies to everything posted on MathemAct - reports, comments, ratings, flags,
    and other contributions - as well as messages sent through contact details listed on the site.
    It applies to everyone, including moderators.
  </p>

  <h2>What we expect</h2>

  <dl class="items">
    <dt>Discuss the work, not the person</dt>
    <dd>
      <i>“I don’t think this verification rules out the main source of error”</i> is useful criticism.<br>
      <i>“Anyone who trusts a chatbot with a proof is not a serious mathematician”</i> is not.<br>
      Try to make criticism something another person can respond to.
    </dd>

    <dt>Assume good faith</dt>
    <dd>
      People here are describing their own experiences, often including things that went wrong.
      You can question a claim, ask how something was checked, or ask for more evidence. But do
      not start from the assumption that someone is lying or acting dishonestly without a reason.
    </dd>

    <dt>Respect pseudonyms</dt>
    <dd>
      Some contributors may not want their activity on this site connected to their professional
      identity.<br>
      Do not try to identify pseudonymous contributors, speculate publicly about who they are,
      or reveal information that could identify them.
    </dd>

    <dt>Do not weaponise a disclosure</dt>
    <dd>
      People may share things here that they would not normally disclose publicly: extensive AI use,
      failed attempts, mistakes, or uncertain results.<br>
      Do not use information disclosed on MathemAct to target someone professionally or personally
      - for example in hiring, refereeing, grant discussions, or public attacks.
    </dd>

    <dt>Respect other people's material</dt>
    <dd>
      Only post material that you have the right to share.
      Before posting transcripts, remove unpublished work, private correspondence, referee reports,
      or other material belonging to someone who has not agreed to its publication.
    </dd>
  </dl>

  <h2>What is not acceptable</h2>

  <p>The following is not allowed:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>harassment, intimidation, threats, or sustained personal hostility;</li>
    <li>attempts to identify pseudonymous contributors or reveal someone's private information;</li>
    <li>demeaning remarks based on nationality, gender, religion, disability, age, institution, career stage, or similar personal characteristics;</li>
    <li>fabricated reports, transcripts, outcomes, or verification steps;</li>
    <li>spam or promotion presented as a genuine contribution;</li>
    <li>publishing private or unpublished material without permission.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Strong disagreement is not a violation. Neither is reporting that a popular tool failed, that
    an unpopular one worked, or that you disagree with the prevailing view.
  </p>

  <h2>Flagging content</h2>

  <p>
    Reports, claims, and comments can be flagged from the page where they appear.
  </p>
  
  <p>
    MathemAct does not pre-moderate contributions, so flags are the main way potential problems
    are brought to moderators' attention. If the issue concerns a moderator, or you prefer not to
    use the site, you can write to <a href={`mailto:${SITE.contactEmail}`}>{SITE.contactEmail}</a>.
  </p>

  <p>
    When flagging something, tell us what you are concerned about and where it appears. You do not need to justify why you found it upsetting or offensive.
  </p>

  <h2>What happens after a flag</h2>

  <p>
    Moderation is volunteer-run, so decisions may take a few days.
  </p>

  <p>
    Content that appears likely to cause serious harm may be temporarily hidden while it is reviewed.
    Temporary hiding does not mean that a violation has been found.
  </p>

  <p>
    When a decision is made, both the author and the person who flagged the content receive a short
    explanation under moderation decisions on their account pages. The identity of the person who
    submitted the flag is not disclosed, and decisions are presented as decisions of the site rather
    than of an individual moderator.
  </p>

  <p>
    Moderators also keep an internal record of decisions, including what was decided and why.
  </p>

  <p>Depending on the situation:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>the content may remain unchanged;</li>
    <li>the content may be hidden;</li>
    <li>an account may be suspended;</li>
    <li>in serious cases, an account may be removed.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Hidden content is preserved and can be restored. Authors can also edit hidden reports where
    appropriate.
  </p>

  <p>
    If action is taken against your content or account, you will be told which rule was applied and why.
    You can ask for the decision to be reconsidered.
  </p>

  <p>
    Attempts to deanonymise contributors and serious harassment may lead to action without a prior warning.
  </p>

  <h2>Account suspension</h2>

  <p>
    Suspension is used when the problem is not a single contribution but an ongoing pattern, such as
    repeated spam or sustained hostility toward other contributors.
  </p>

  <p>
    Being wrong, posting a negative result, or making one poorly judged contribution is not in itself
    grounds for suspension.
  </p>

  <p>
    A suspended account still exists. You can sign in, read the site, manage your profile and password,
    and request deletion of your account. You cannot post, comment, answer claims, submit reports, flag
    content, or otherwise make new contributions while the suspension is active.
  </p>

  <p>Existing contributions normally stay visible unless there is a separate reason to moderate them.</p>

  <p>
    If your account is suspended, the reason will appear under moderation decisions on your account page.
    Suspensions are not shown publicly: there is no public badge or list of suspended users.
  </p>

  <p>
    You can appeal a suspension by writing to 
    <a href={`mailto:${SITE.contactEmail}`}>{SITE.contactEmail}</a>. Where possible, an appeal will be
    reviewed by a different moderator. Suspensions can be lifted, and the reason for doing so will also
    be recorded.
  </p>

  <p>
    Moderators cannot suspend themselves or other moderators through the normal moderation interface.
  </p>

  <h2>Changes to this code</h2>

  <p>This code will evolve as MathemAct encounters situations we did not anticipate.</p>
  
  <p>
    Changes are dated and recorded in the
    <a href={SITE.repository}>public repository</a>. The
    <a href={path('/privacy/')}>privacy notice</a> explains what information the site stores and
    what happens to your contributions if you leave.
  </p>
</Page>

